WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.900
It's a shocking death that will spark a war — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.377
how do the final moments of House&nbsp;
of the Dragon differ from the book?

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.840
The final episode of Season 1 of House of&nbsp;
the Dragon feels like a powder keg about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.780
to be ignited. After Aegon II is crowned king at&nbsp;
breakneck speed after the death of King Viserys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.180
events are set in motion that will soon see&nbsp;
Westeros engulfed in war. Obviously, Viserys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.640
had named his daughter Rhaenyra as heir, and this&nbsp;
rapid move to usurp her claim by Alicent Hightower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.620
and her father, Otto, has already created a&nbsp;
schism in the lords and ladies of Westeros.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.320
However, the coronation of Aegon II is not without&nbsp;
its hiccups, as Princess Rhaenys manages to free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.540
her dragon Meleys, which she uses to crash&nbsp;
through the floor of the Dragon Pit of King's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.400
Landing. A rather dramatic way of establishing&nbsp;
where Rhaenys' loyalties may lie, Episode 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.800
picks up in the immediate aftermath, with Rhaenys&nbsp;
explaining to Rhaenyra what has just transpired.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.341
"The Greens are coming for you,&nbsp;
Rhaenyra. And for your children."

00:01:00.341 --> 00:01:05.400
Quickly summoning her council, as well as her&nbsp;
husband and uncle Daemon, Rhaenyra shows far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.720
restraint than her compatriots, who begin to plan&nbsp;
a war of succession pretty much straight away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.940
Unfortunately, Rhaenyra's calm and collective&nbsp;
demeanor isn't exactly rewarded later in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.220
episode, as her careful approach to the conflict&nbsp;
is shattered by the heedless actions of children.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.800
Acknowledging that the faction now known as&nbsp;
the Greens has far more soldiers and allies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.280
Rhaenyra dispatches messengers&nbsp;
to every corner of Westeros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.840
hoping to shore up support. After ordering&nbsp;
her two children, Jacaerys and Lucerys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.940
to head to Winterfell and Storm's End, the two&nbsp;
brothers take to their dragons. Lucerys is sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.100
to Storm's End to treat with Borros Baratheon, but&nbsp;
he quickly realizes that somebody has beaten him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.780
there. Coming face-to-face with his one-eyed&nbsp;
uncle Aemond, Lucerys is unable to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.100
Borros to lend his support to his family's&nbsp;
side, who are now known as the "Blacks."

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.480
"Go home, pup. And tell your mother&nbsp;
that the Lord of Storm's End is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.182
some dog she can whistle up at&nbsp;
need to set against her foes."

00:02:01.182 --> 00:02:06.600
Realizing that he is ill-prepared for this&nbsp;
negotiation, Lucerys attempts to leave. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.060
he is able to depart, however, Aemond demands&nbsp;
that Lucerys cut out his own eye to make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.740
for the wound Lucerys had inflicted on Aemond in&nbsp;
their youth. However, Borros doesn't particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:22.620
care for this bluster and allows Lucerys to leave.&nbsp;
Taking to his young dragon Arrax, who is greatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.140
unsettled by the ongoing intense storm, Lucerys&nbsp;
begins to fly back to Dragonstone. Soon, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.840
he realizes that Aemond is in pursuit with&nbsp;
Vhagar, the largest and most fearsome dragon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.780
in Westeros. Aemond taunts Lucerys from atop&nbsp;
his dragon, still demanding his eye — but both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.436
children have trouble controlling their dragons&nbsp;
on account of them being untested dragonriders.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.000
As Aemond's pursuit of Lucerys becomes more&nbsp;
and more dire, Lucerys' dragon Arrax is visibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.640
shaken, and both dragons appear to be letting&nbsp;
their primal instincts take over — despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.560
protests of both riders. As the hunter,&nbsp;
Vhagar's size is incredibly apparent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.420
though this doesn't stop Arrax from blasting&nbsp;
the ancient dragon with fire. This in turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.560
causes Vhagar to release its full&nbsp;
fury upon both Arrax and Lucerys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.200
and in an instant both dragon and rider are&nbsp;
killed with a ravenous bite from Vhagar.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.798
"Vhagar, no! No!"

00:03:20.654 --> 00:03:26.040
This comes as a surprise to Aemond, who realizes&nbsp;
that his actions will have deadly consequences.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:32.220
As shocking as this scene was for viewers, this&nbsp;
scene actually has far more nuance than what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.900
presented in Fire and Blood. In the grand scheme&nbsp;
of things, this scene is considered a defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.880
moment in the conflict known as the "Dance of&nbsp;
Dragons," and several different variations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:47.400
the story are told regarding what happened, but&nbsp;
all agreed upon one thing — Lucerys and Arrax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.660
were tragically under-matched against Aemond&nbsp;
and Vhagar. And while you see that Aemond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:56.520
is clearly disturbed by what happened in the&nbsp;
show, that's not how it goes down in the book.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.820
This is on account of the nature of&nbsp;
the way Fire and Blood is written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.670
as a kind of historical document with unreliable&nbsp;
narrators. The book portrays the fight as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:11.700
sided and very much intentional, with Aemond&nbsp;
motivated primarily by revenge. There are several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.380
stories told about the aftermath of the fight,&nbsp;
with some saying that Arrax's head washed ashore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.780
others saying that both Lucerys and Arrax were&nbsp;
swallowed whole, and one suggesting that Lucerys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.940
actually survived but suffered from permanent&nbsp;
amnesia. Either way, Lucerys is never found.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.920
This change in House of the Dragon&nbsp;
makes for a much more dynamic story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.140
which is important considering this moment sparks&nbsp;
the Targaryen civil war in Westeros. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.480
Rhaenyra didn't want to strike the first blow, she&nbsp;
was given her wish — albeit at a terrible price.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.940
To see Aemond realize his actions will have&nbsp;
disastrous consequences clearly shines a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:52.260
light on the story, and it’ll be interesting to&nbsp;
see how he’ll report the outcome to his family.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.620
In an interview with Variety,&nbsp;
series co-creator Ryan Condal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.920
was asked about why they decided&nbsp;
to make Lucerys' death an accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.600
and not intentional. He clarified that&nbsp;
even though it is presented as an accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.540
Aemond still jumped onto his dragon and chased&nbsp;
his nephew, so it isn't as clear-cut as a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.400
mistake. Condal then explained how history&nbsp;
itself is messy, and how the historians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.140
storytellers of Westeros couldn't actually know&nbsp;
what was going through Aemond's head. He added:

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.080
"This is a war of many cuts that lead to a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.660
really bloody wound. It adds complexity and nuance&nbsp;
to the character that's potentially interesting."

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.180
Discussing the future of&nbsp;
Aemond's story, Condal added:

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:36.780
"There's lots of runway to go on with Aemond as a&nbsp;
character and the story of the Dance. This is his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:42.540
first act as a dragon rider and a warrior and it's&nbsp;
gone very wrong. Now what happens as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.340
and how does he respond? Those are the&nbsp;
questions I'm interested in as a dramatist."

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:52.200
Ultimately, this departure from the source&nbsp;
material definitely adds yet another layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.600
to the upcoming conflict and the way both sides&nbsp;
will respond — and shape the history of Westeros.

